From 77ed4c2f638c9b2c826fe28ffc5a8add48280c4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ben Walker <ben@nvidia.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Aug 2024 09:42:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/21] sock: All sockets must be in groups

g_admin_poll_group_mutex was a plain, non-recursive mutex, but
nvme_tcp_qpair_process_completions() locks it around
spdk_sock_group_poll() on the shared admin socket group. If that poll
surfaces a socket error for the qpair currently being polled (a
TLS/PSK handshake failure, for example), pdu_write_done() synchronously
disconnects the qpair via nvme_tcp_ctrlr_disconnect_qpair(), which
calls nvme_tcp_qpair_put_sock_group() and tries to lock the same mutex
again on the same thread. That's a permanent self-deadlock:
bdev_nvme_attach_controller with a mismatched PSK wedges the reactor
instead of failing the RPC.

Make the mutex recursive, initialized once via pthread_once, so the
reentrant same-thread relock succeeds instead of deadlocking. This
preserves the mutex's actual job - serializing concurrent
spdk_sock_group_poll() calls on the shared admin group from different
threads - without weakening it; PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE is already the
pattern nvme_ctrlr.c and keyring.c use for the same class of
reentrant-lock problem.

Change-Id: I5cd0166e799b2b24f04dffe4cb61eb7587ac5b81
Signed-off-by: Ben Walker <ben@nvidia.com>
